Client and Project Summary
The Gaming Room was a hypothetical client requesting the design of a specialized software application. Their primary goal was to create a C++ system capable of managing customer game rental histories and loyalty points. The software is needed to support both employees and customers - offering features such as searching and viewing game details, calculating rental costs, managing user accounts, and updating loyalty balances accurately. In short, the client required a reliable, efficient, and user-friendly database management and transaction processing system for a video game rental business.
Design and Reflection
I believe I excelled in the System Architecture and Component Design section. I clearly defined key classes - such as Customer, Game, Transaction, and LoyaltyProgram - and described their relationships using UML diagrams. This modular breakdown made the complex requirements manageable and provided a solid blueprint for implementation. Additionally, the Data Dictionary was thorough and helped standardize data types and constraints for fields such as GameID, RentalPeriod, and LoyaltyPoints.
What part of the design process helped the most?
Working through UML class diagrams and pseudocode for core algorithms like calculating late fees and loyalty points was extremely valuable. These artifacts helped identify potential logic gaps before any code was written, making the coding process more structured and less prone to trial-and-error.
What would I revise or improve?
I would improve the Testing and Quality Assurance Plan. While unit testing was addressed, I would expand it with more detailed integration and user acceptance testing scenarios. Edge cases like “renting with zero available copies” or “loyalty point overflow” would ensure greater reliability and user satisfaction.
How did I interpret and implement user needs?
I designed with the client’s business goals, efficiency and customer satisfaction - at the forefront. For example, I implemented a minimal-click user interface and created a dedicated LoyaltyProgram class to automate customer rewards. Considering user needs is crucial because even the most technical sound system fails if it isn’t intuitive or valuable to its users.
How did I approach software design, and what would I do in the future?
My approach followed a top-down, Object-Oriented Analysis and Design methodology, starting with requirements gathering and ending with testing. In future projects, I plan to integrate Behavior-Driven Development principles - using “Given-When-Then” user stories - and employ more detailed State Diagrams to model lifecycle behaviors. These strategies would improve clarity, traceability, and overall system robustness.
